1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:01,900
On the world's highest mountain,

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:05,080
there is a body climbers have walked past for 30 years,

3
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:06,920
using a name that was never his.

4
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:13,120
May 10th, 1996. Three men radio their base camp with a summit claim.

5
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:15,400
Within hours, all three are dead.

6
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:20,100
One of their bodies became the most famous landmark on Mount Everest.

7
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:22,380
Climbers named him for his boots,

8
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:25,920
because for 30 years, nobody was sure of anything else.

9
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:31,880
This year, that changed. And the answer means the man everyone thought they'd found is

10
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:36,380
still missing. This is the story of everybody still on Everest.

11
00:00:36,940 --> 00:00:38,820
The ones with names, the ones without,

12
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:40,520
and the one nobody had right.

13
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:43,020
You're watching Mind Wired.

14
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:46,980
This is the story of the bodies still on Mount Everest,

15
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:48,780
the men who never came down,

16
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:51,700
and the 30 -year mistake that just got corrected.

17
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:55,980
Chapter 1. The Mountain That Keeps Its Dead.

18
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:01,680
The most cited estimate says more than 200 bodies remain on Mount Everest today.

19
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:03,980
Nobody has ever actually counted them.

20
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,540
That figure traces back to a single 2015 magazine article.

21
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:10,800
Every outlet since has repeated it.

22
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:13,660
What's actually confirmed is smaller and stranger.

23
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:17,920
Total recorded deaths on Everest since 1922 now exceed 300,

24
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:21,460
and more than half of those who died were Nepali guides and workers,

25
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:23,460
not the climbers whose names make headlines.

26
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:29,700
Above 8 ,000 meters, the air holds barely a third of the oxygen it does at sea level.

27
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:33,420
Doctors call it the death zone because the human body doesn't adapt there.

28
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:37,480
It just runs out. The cold does something else.

29
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:39,960
At routine temperatures of minus 40,

30
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:41,680
a body doesn't decompose.

31
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:45,960
It mummifies, preserved by the same mountain that killed it.

32
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:48,500
Recovering even one of them costs,

33
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:54,100
on average, between $40 ,000 and $80 ,000 and takes 6 to 10 climbers,

34
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:58,500
days of work, dragging dead weight down terrain that can barely hold the living.

35
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:02,820
No helicopter can reliably reach most of them.

36
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:07,840
Routine rescue flights stop around Camp 2 at roughly 6 ,400 meters.

37
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:13,300
The single highest altitude helicopter rescue ever performed happened once in perfect

38
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:17,500
conditions in 2013. It has never been repeated on demand.

39
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:23,660
Commercial guided climbing on Everest is usually traced to one man with almost no mountaineering

40
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:27,000
background. Dick Bass, a Utah ski resort owner,

41
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:31,300
reached the summit in 1985 with a professional guide doing most of the work.

42
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:35,140
His book about it is widely credited with turning Everest into a business.

43
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:39,440
By 1996, that business was booming.

44
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:42,080
Scott Fisher's company, Mountain Madness,

45
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:44,880
and Rob Hall's company, Adventure Consultants,

46
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,040
were both guiding paying clients to the top.

47
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:50,180
Hall's rate, $65 ,000 a head,

48
00:02:50,220 --> 00:02:52,660
was the highest of any operator on the mountain.

49
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:55,660
In September of last year,

50
00:02:55,940 --> 00:03:01,160
Nepal raised its climbing permit fee again to $15 ,000 per person for the spring

51
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:06,220
season, a 36 % jump. Officials said part of the reason was

52
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:08,620
the mounting cost of getting bodies off the mountain.

53
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:14,480
Chapter 2, The Night Everyone Remembers May 10,

54
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:19,780
1996. Rob Hall's team and Scott Fisher's team are both pushing for the summit on the

55
00:03:19,780 --> 00:03:22,760
same day on the mountain south side in Nepal.

56
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:28,380
Bottlenecks at two fixed rope sections cost the group nearly two hours they didn't have.

57
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:33,600
By early afternoon, Hall's own turnaround deadline has already passed and climbers are

58
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:37,460
still below the top. Around 5 .30 in the afternoon,

59
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,180
the weather breaks completely,

60
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:43,160
winds over 70 knots, visibility near zero.

61
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:48,600
Climbers who are still descending are now doing it blind in a blizzard above 8 ,000 meters.

62
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:54,440
Doug Hanson, a postal worker from Washington State on his second attempt to summit for

63
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:56,740
Hall's company, disappears during the descent.

64
00:03:57,240 --> 00:03:58,540
His body has never been found.

65
00:04:00,020 --> 00:04:02,060
Andy Harris, one of Hall's guides,

66
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:04,380
climbs back up into the storm to try to help.

67
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:07,860
He disappears too. His body has never been found either.

68
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:11,380
Rob Hall himself survives the night on the mountain,

69
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:13,160
radioing base camp at dawn,

70
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:16,060
alive, but no longer able to manage his own ropes.

71
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:19,480
That afternoon, his last call is to his pregnant wife,

72
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:22,620
back home. He dies of exposure shortly after.

73
00:04:24,080 --> 00:04:26,120
Rob Hall's body remains on Everest today.

74
00:04:26,540 --> 00:04:30,000
His widow has said publicly that she wants him left exactly where he is.

75
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:33,040
Scott Fisher dies the same night,

76
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:35,080
found frozen by a fellow climber.

77
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:37,180
His body also remains on the mountain.

78
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:43,240
Yasuko Namba, a Japanese client who had just become the oldest woman ever to summit Everest,

79
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:44,920
dies on the South Colonel.

80
00:04:45,060 --> 00:04:49,140
A recovery funded by her husband brought her remains home to Japan the following year,

81
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:52,300
one of the very few from that night who ever left the mountain.

82
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:56,260
No government inquiry was ever opened into that night.

83
00:04:56,780 --> 00:05:01,620
What exists instead are two competing first -hand accounts written by two different survivors

84
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:04,960
who disagree with each other about what happened and who's to blame.

85
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:07,480
Neither one is the official record.

86
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:09,660
Both are just what two people remember.

87
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:13,180
Eight people died on Everest that single week.

88
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:15,540
Four of them are the names everyone remembers.

89
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:20,040
The other four died on the other side of the mountain and almost nobody talks about them

90
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:25,980
at all. Chapter 3, The Night Nobody Talks About On

91
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:27,080
the north side of Everest,

92
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:32,220
in Tibet, a six -man team from India's Indo -Tibetan border police is making its own

93
00:05:32,220 --> 00:05:34,440
summit attempt on the exact same day.

94
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:38,360
Three of the six turn back as conditions worsen.

95
00:05:38,820 --> 00:05:41,640
Three keep climbing. Team leader Swang Samanla,

96
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:43,600
who had already summited once before.

97
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:49,500
Lance Naik Dorje Morup, 48 years old and head constable Swang Palja,

98
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:53,260
28. Before he left for the climb,

99
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:57,460
Morup told his wife he'd come back a hero and asked her to meet him at the airport.

100
00:05:58,100 --> 00:06:01,060
He'd already climbed the tallest peak in his home region,

101
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:04,220
and this was supposed to be the one that mattered.

102
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:07,220
Around 5 .30 in the afternoon,

103
00:06:07,620 --> 00:06:10,660
Beijing time, the three men radio down that they've reached the top.

104
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:14,320
A Chinese summit certificate later records the exact time for Morup,

105
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:19,940
5 .30 p .m., May 10th. One detail may have cost them everything.

106
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:24,180
Samanla reportedly stayed behind on the summit to perform a religious ceremony,

107
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:25,880
taking time the group didn't have.

108
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:30,760
The descent starts late. The same storm that's about to kill four people on the other

109
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:32,860
side of the mountain catches them in the open.

110
00:06:34,620 --> 00:06:36,180
None of the three make it back to High Camp.

111
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:38,020
All three die in the blizzard.

112
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:40,680
On the descent, on the north side of Everest,

113
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:44,400
the same night Rob Hall and Scott Fisher are dying on the south side.

114
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:48,380
Eight people died on Everest in that single storm.

115
00:06:48,860 --> 00:06:51,080
Most of the world only ever heard about four of them.

116
00:06:52,460 --> 00:06:56,520
One of those three Indian climbers would go on to become the most photographed dead body

117
00:06:56,520 --> 00:07:01,120
on Mount Everest, known to thousands of climbers by a name that had nothing to do with

118
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:04,100
who he actually was. Chapter four,

119
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:06,740
30 years, one wrong name.

120
00:07:08,260 --> 00:07:13,300
A body in a small limestone alcove at roughly 8 ,500 meters on Everest's northeast

121
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:17,820
ridge was first filmed by a British documentary crew in May of 1996.

122
00:07:18,420 --> 00:07:20,940
All the film crew could say at the time was that he was from India.

123
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:26,460
Climbers started calling him Green Boots for the bright green mountaineering boots still

124
00:07:26,460 --> 00:07:28,980
on his feet. For the next 30 years,

125
00:07:29,340 --> 00:07:32,240
that nickname was the only thing about him nobody argued over.

126
00:07:33,560 --> 00:07:38,060
The popular assumption, repeated in trekking blogs and climbing guides for three decades,

127
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:40,500
was that Green Boots was Tzuang Palja,

128
00:07:40,780 --> 00:07:42,420
the 28 -year -old head constable.

129
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:46,200
But that assumption had a problem nobody was checking.

130
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:51,940
A written account from 1997 by one of the expedition's own officers described a

131
00:07:51,940 --> 00:07:56,700
very different man struggling with frostbitten hands on the descent and a search party

132
00:07:56,700 --> 00:08:01,820
finding his body near a boulder close to Camp Six with his clothing and packs still intact.

133
00:08:02,220 --> 00:08:03,920
That account named Morup,

134
00:08:04,420 --> 00:08:09,140
not Palja. It had been sitting there in a mountaineering journal the entire time.

135
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:15,720
In 2014, a Chinese expedition managing the route moved the body to a less visible spot

136
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:20,220
off the main path. Climbers who'd seen Green Boots for years suddenly stopped seeing

137
00:08:20,220 --> 00:08:25,440
him at all. This year, India's Indo -Tibetan border police finally settled

138
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:29,240
it. According to tender documents reviewed by international wire reporters,

139
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:34,840
the force says it collected DNA samples back in 2024 and that testing ahead of a planned

140
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:37,100
recovery mission confirmed the identity.

141
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:39,680
Here's the honest complication.

142
00:08:40,260 --> 00:08:42,560
The lab report itself has never been made public.

143
00:08:43,100 --> 00:08:47,000
No city, no scientist, no case file has been named anywhere.

144
00:08:47,700 --> 00:08:51,140
Other reporting says the identification actually rests on gear,

145
00:08:51,640 --> 00:08:56,960
radio logs, and decades -old eyewitness testimony with full DNA testing only happening

146
00:08:56,960 --> 00:08:58,840
once the body is finally brought home.

147
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:02,000
Either way, the conclusion is the same.

148
00:09:02,660 --> 00:09:04,220
Green Boots was never to Swang Palja.

149
00:09:04,540 --> 00:09:08,540
He was Dorje Morup, the 48 -year -old who told his wife he'd come back a hero.

150
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:14,700
Which means the man everyone spent 30 years calling Green Boots has finally been named.

151
00:09:15,120 --> 00:09:16,440
And the man they thought he was,

152
00:09:16,860 --> 00:09:20,560
Tsawang Palja, is still somewhere on that mountain unaccounted for.

153
00:09:21,880 --> 00:09:24,020
Morup's wife, Conchok Yangskit,

154
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:27,940
is in her mid -70s now. She gave her first public interview this year.

155
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:42,500
India's government has now put out a formal tender for a recovery mission targeting a

156
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:46,540
window between June and September of this year aiming to bring Morup's remains to Delhi

157
00:09:46,540 --> 00:09:51,180
by October. His son says the family plans a funeral near a memorial park in Leh,

158
00:09:51,540 --> 00:09:55,660
and wants his father and his two teammates formally recognized as martyrs.

159
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:58,440
Thirty years, one mountain,

160
00:09:58,900 --> 00:10:00,560
one name finally corrected,

161
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:05,400
and one family still waiting for the mountain to give back a different man entirely.

162
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:12,440
Chapter 5, The One They Never Found Green Boots isn't the only identity

163
00:10:12,440 --> 00:10:13,500
mystery on this mountain.

164
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:15,360
It isn't even the oldest one.

165
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:21,900
June 8th, 1924 George Mallory and Andrew Irvine on Britain's third Everest

166
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:25,660
expedition are last seen climbing toward the summit on the northeast ridge.

167
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:31,260
Then they vanish. Whether either man reached the top 29 years before

168
00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:36,060
Everest's first confirmed ascent remains one of mountaineering's oldest open questions.

169
00:10:37,580 --> 00:10:42,740
May 1st, 1999 A research expedition finds Mallory's body 75

170
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:44,160
years after he disappeared.

171
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:47,440
Personal effects on the body help confirm who he is.

172
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:48,940
There's no camera on him.

173
00:10:50,140 --> 00:10:53,020
That camera matters more than almost anything else on this list.

174
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:55,580
Irvine was believed to be carrying it.

175
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,640
If it still exists, and if the film could somehow still be developed,

176
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:03,260
it could theoretically prove whether either man reached the summit.

177
00:11:03,980 --> 00:11:06,960
It has never been found. Then,

178
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:11,120
in September of 2024, a hundred years after they vanished,

179
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:12,780
a film crew finds a boot,

180
00:11:13,120 --> 00:11:16,540
a partial foot, and a sock embroidered with the initials A -C.

181
00:11:17,380 --> 00:11:20,400
Irvine, on a glacier below the mountain's north face.

182
00:11:21,860 --> 00:11:24,160
Irvine's Great Meese was the first family member told.

183
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:27,140
The family has volunteered DNA to help confirm it.

184
00:11:27,460 --> 00:11:29,040
The camera still hasn't turned up.

185
00:11:30,340 --> 00:11:34,840
A hundred years apart, two different families have now waited for the same mountain to

186
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:36,480
finally answer the same question.

187
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:39,920
Who was this, really? And what were they still carrying?

188
00:11:41,620 --> 00:11:46,300
Chapter 6 The ones they couldn't save October 2nd,

189
00:11:46,480 --> 00:11:48,900
1979. Hannah -Law Schmatz,

190
00:11:49,140 --> 00:11:52,500
a German climber, becomes the fourth woman ever to summit Everest.

191
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:56,360
On the way down, roughly a hundred meters above Camp 4,

192
00:11:56,740 --> 00:11:58,660
exhaustion and exposure kill her.

193
00:11:59,960 --> 00:12:01,840
Her body stayed visible for years,

194
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:05,400
seated, held roughly in place by her own climbing rope,

195
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:07,160
in view of anyone passing by.

196
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:11,000
A climber is believed to have cut the rope during high winds in 1982.

197
00:12:11,760 --> 00:12:15,140
Another climber still spotted remains as late as 1985.

198
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:18,840
Eventually, the wind alone finished the job.

199
00:12:20,380 --> 00:12:24,620
In October of 1984, a Nepalese police team tried to recover her remains.

200
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:28,920
A police inspector and a sherpa guide both fell to their deaths during the attempt.

201
00:12:29,380 --> 00:12:32,160
The mountain took two more lives trying to bring back one.

202
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:36,680
May 15th, 2006. David Sharp,

203
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:41,480
a British climber, is dying alone in a small rock alcove on the northeast ridge,

204
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:43,780
the same general area as Green Boots.

205
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:48,100
Somewhere between 30 and 40 other climbers pass him over the following day,

206
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:53,300
on their way up or down. It wasn't a flat story of everyone simply

207
00:12:53,300 --> 00:12:56,720
ignoring him. Some sherpa guides and climbers did try,

208
00:12:56,900 --> 00:13:00,340
giving him oxygen, attempting to move him before continuing on.

209
00:13:00,680 --> 00:13:04,860
He was already severely frostbitten and immobile by the time most of them reached him.

210
00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:07,500
One climber who passed him,

211
00:13:07,640 --> 00:13:11,220
in later interviews, said his team radioed their expedition leader,

212
00:13:11,220 --> 00:13:13,520
and was told there was nothing to be done.

213
00:13:13,920 --> 00:13:18,060
He has since said, the altitude may have affected his memory of exactly what was said.

214
00:13:18,380 --> 00:13:19,780
Whatever the precise truth,

215
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:22,220
David Sharp died on the mountain that day.

216
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:26,220
Sir Edmund Hillary, the first man to ever summon Everest,

217
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:27,960
was furious when he heard,

218
00:13:29,180 --> 00:13:33,320
on my expedition, there was no way that you would have left a man under a rock to die.

219
00:13:33,940 --> 00:13:37,360
Human life is far more important than just getting to the top of a mountain,

220
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:42,920
end quote. One real, uncomfortable statistic followed that debate.

221
00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:47,880
Expeditions that had a death on a commercial trip still pushed onto the summit afterward,

222
00:13:48,220 --> 00:13:51,740
80 .6 % of the time. Non -commercial expeditions,

223
00:13:52,120 --> 00:13:54,240
only 37 .8 % of the time.

224
00:13:56,320 --> 00:13:57,940
Chapter 7, still happening.

225
00:13:59,360 --> 00:14:00,560
This isn't a closed story.

226
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,180
This spring, Everest had a record season,

227
00:14:03,580 --> 00:14:06,900
over a thousand summits, and it still killed five people.

228
00:14:08,320 --> 00:14:12,900
Lakpa Dendi Sherpa, 52, fell before he even reached the mountain proper.

229
00:14:13,580 --> 00:14:15,720
B .J. Gimaya Bishwakama, 35,

230
00:14:16,320 --> 00:14:18,460
the first Dalit climber ever to summit Everest,

231
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:21,400
died of suspected heart failure in the Cumbu Icefall.

232
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:24,740
Fura Galjan Sherpa, barely into his 20s,

233
00:14:24,840 --> 00:14:26,580
fell into a crevasse on the lot's face.

234
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:30,780
Two more climbers from India died descending after their own successful summits.

235
00:14:32,220 --> 00:14:34,180
After those deaths, Nepal's army,

236
00:14:34,480 --> 00:14:35,800
working with local authorities,

237
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:38,280
started organising a new clean -up campaign,

238
00:14:38,600 --> 00:14:42,380
aiming to bring down at least five more bodies from Everest and the neighbouring peak,

239
00:14:42,880 --> 00:14:45,700
Nupsay, along with the usual tonnes of abandoned gear.

240
00:14:47,240 --> 00:14:50,440
Since these organised army campaigns began in 2019,

241
00:14:50,920 --> 00:14:53,300
they've recovered a combined 14 bodies,

242
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:58,320
several sets of skeletal remains and roughly 119 tonnes of waste.

243
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
The mountain is still giving its dead back,

244
00:15:01,260 --> 00:15:06,340
a handful at a time. Nepal's parliament is now moving toward a law that

245
00:15:06,340 --> 00:15:10,420
would require every expedition to carry insurance covering the cost of bringing bodies

246
00:15:10,420 --> 00:15:12,360
home, not just rescuing the living.

247
00:15:12,900 --> 00:15:14,560
It's past one house of parliament.

248
00:15:15,120 --> 00:15:18,600
It isn't law yet. For a hundred years,

249
00:15:18,800 --> 00:15:21,520
this mountain has taken people and kept most of them.

250
00:15:22,060 --> 00:15:26,460
Every so often, it corrects one small mistake and hands one name back.

251
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:30,560
What stays with me about this story isn't the number.

252
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:34,580
200, 300. It's an estimate either way.

253
00:15:35,840 --> 00:15:40,400
It's that a body most of the world only ever knew as a nickname had a wife in Ladakh,

254
00:15:40,700 --> 00:15:44,920
still waking up some mornings expecting a knock at the door 30 years later.

255
00:15:46,300 --> 00:15:50,160
Every one of these people had a name before the mountain took it away from them.

256
00:15:50,560 --> 00:15:55,340
Getting even one of those names right again 30 years late is apparently the best any

